\ts11\trgaph15\trleft-45\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalc\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \clcbpat8\cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8730\clcbpatraw8 \cellx8685\row }\pard 
\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\cf5\insrsid5067159 
\par 
\par }{\cf18\insrsid5067159 cdecl and __stdcall just tells the compiler whether the called function or the calling function
\par cleans up the stack. In __stdcall calling convention, the called function cleans up the stack when
\par it is about to return. So if it is called in a bunch of different places, all of those calls do not need
\par to extra code to clean up the stack after the function call.
\par In __cdecl calling convention, it is the caller function that is responsible for cleaning the stack,
\par so every function call must also need to include extra code to clean up the stack after the
\par function call.
\par }{\insrsid5067159 * erase window function expmanation 5marks
\par * Clipboard Working 3mraks
\par }{\cf18\insrsid5067159 We can use it for copying the data from one file to the other in same format.e.g from notepad to
\par MS Word}{\cf1\insrsid5067159 .}{\cf18\insrsid5067159 
\par }{\insrsid5067159 Q2: write down complete syntax of "getDC" function?(2)
\par }{\cf18\insrsid5067159 The system retrieves a device context from the cache whenever an application calls the GetDC
\par or BeginPaint function; the system returns the
\par DC to the cache when the application subsequently calls the ReleaseDC or EndPaint
\par function.
